WEBVTT

00:00:00.064 --> 00:00:03.817
PRIMARY KEY and UNIQUE are related, but they are not the same thing.

00:00:03.817 --> 00:00:07.172
The primary key is the table's official row identity and cannot be NULL.

00:00:07.172 --> 00:00:12.162
A table has one primary-key constraint, although that key may contain multiple columns.

00:00:12.162 --> 00:00:15.032
UNIQUE protects other candidate keys or business rules.

00:00:15.032 --> 00:00:18.692
A table can have several unique constraints.

00:00:18.692 --> 00:00:21.487
PostgreSQL also has specific behavior around NULL values in unique constraints,

00:00:21.487 --> 00:00:26.332
so do not assume a UNIQUE column behaves exactly like a primary key.

00:00:26.332 --> 00:00:28.902
In the clinic example, patient ID is the primary key,

00:00:28.902 --> 00:00:34.612
while MRN remains unique because duplicate medical record numbers would still be a serious data-quality

00:00:34.612 --> 00:00:36.527
problem. A useful question is: if the system-generated ID is already unique,

00:00:36.527 --> 00:00:41.197
which business identifiers still need independent protection?

00:00:41.197 --> 00:00:43.022
Keys are not merely technical identifiers.

00:00:43.022 --> 00:00:47.282
A primary key protects row identity, a unique constraint protects a business rule,

00:00:47.282 --> 00:00:49.492
and a foreign key protects a relationship.

00:00:49.492 --> 00:00:52.612
In production systems, surrogate keys are often convenient,

00:00:52.612 --> 00:00:57.351
but important natural identifiers may still need their own uniqueness rule.
